=== Part 1: O(N) + cubic anisotropy, one loop, eps = 1 ===
  N = 2: y_v at the O(N) point = -0.2000 (IRRELEVANT -> O(N) emerges)
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, +0.15): -> (u, v) = (0.6000, 0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.6000, v = 0]
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, -0.10): -> (u, v) = (0.6000, -0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.6000, v = 0]
     UV (u, v) = (0.05, +0.30): -> (u, v) = (0.6000, 0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.6000, v = 0]
  N = 3: y_v at the O(N) point = -0.0909 (IRRELEVANT -> O(N) emerges)
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, +0.15): -> (u, v) = (0.5455, 0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.5455, v = 0]
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, -0.10): RUNAWAY (fluct.-induced 1st order)
     UV (u, v) = (0.05, +0.30): -> (u, v) = (0.5455, 0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.5455, v = 0]
  N = 3.5: y_v at the O(N) point = -0.0435 (IRRELEVANT -> O(N) emerges)
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, +0.15): -> (u, v) = (0.5217, 0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.5217, v = 0]
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, -0.10): RUNAWAY (fluct.-induced 1st order)
     UV (u, v) = (0.05, +0.30): -> (u, v) = (0.5217, 0.0000)   [O(N): u* = 0.5217, v = 0]
  N = 4.5: y_v at the O(N) point = +0.0400 (RELEVANT -> no enhancement)
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, +0.15): -> (u, v) = (0.4444, 0.0741)   [O(N): u* = 0.4800, v = 0]
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, -0.10): RUNAWAY (fluct.-induced 1st order)
     UV (u, v) = (0.05, +0.30): -> (u, v) = (0.4444, 0.0741)   [O(N): u* = 0.4800, v = 0]
  N = 6: y_v at the O(N) point = +0.1429 (RELEVANT -> no enhancement)
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, +0.15): -> (u, v) = (0.3333, 0.2222)   [O(N): u* = 0.4286, v = 0]
     UV (u, v) = (0.2, -0.10): RUNAWAY (fluct.-induced 1st order)
     UV (u, v) = (0.05, +0.30): -> (u, v) = (0.3333, 0.2222)   [O(N): u* = 0.4286, v = 0]
  one-loop N_c = 4; higher-loop / bootstrap estimates N_c ~ 2.9 (SECONDARY): for N = 3 the cubic
  perturbation is in fact weakly RELEVANT (y_v ~ +0.01), i.e. N = 3 is NOT robustly enhanced (contested/marginal)

=== Part 2: 2D Ising at T_c, emergent rotation symmetry (L = 128, Wolff) ===
  isotropic J: ratio C_axis(r) / C_diag(r) at equal |r| (diag points (k,k), |r| = k sqrt 2; axis interpolated)
    |r| =   1.41: C_axis = 0.6636, C_diag = 0.6401, ratio = 1.0368
    |r| =   2.83: C_axis = 0.5514, C_diag = 0.5457, ratio = 1.0103
    |r| =   5.66: C_axis = 0.4665, C_diag = 0.4651, ratio = 1.0030
    |r| =  11.31: C_axis = 0.4004, C_diag = 0.3996, ratio = 1.0018
    |r| =  22.63: C_axis = 0.3486, C_diag = 0.3483, ratio = 1.0007
  uniaxial anisotropy Jy/Jx = 0.5 at its T_c = 1.6410: C along x vs along y at the same r
    r =  2: C_x = 0.6436, C_y = 0.5720, ratio = 1.1252
    r =  4: C_x = 0.5402, C_y = 0.4864, ratio = 1.1107
    r =  8: C_x = 0.4592, C_y = 0.4182, ratio = 1.0980
    r = 16: C_x = 0.3959, C_y = 0.3643, ratio = 1.0867
  -> the anisotropy does NOT wash out: it is redundant (a coordinate rescaling x -> x / xi_x), so the IR is
     rotation-invariant only in rescaled coordinates; the aspect ratio is kept as geometry, not erased.
